{"date": "2026-10-08", "post_id": 1465566, "title": "The Saturn System Sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-8-the-saturn-system-smorgasbord/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> How was the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/\">Saturn</a> system imaged so clearly? Astrophotographer Tom Williams captured such exquisite details due to one night of exceptional atmospheric conditions above the United Kingdom. This means more than a cloudless sky. Pockets of air at different temperatures and densities move around and <a href=\"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/geoopt/refr.html\">bend light</a> as it travels through Earth\u2019s atmosphere, distorting astronomy images. This is called \u201c<a href=\"https://britastro.org/journal_contents_ite/absolute-beginners-no-14-astronomical-seeing\">seeing</a>.\u201d Less atmospheric turbulence means clearer images. The astrophotographer reduced the impact of seeing with the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucky_imaging\">lucky imaging</a> technique: thousands of short-exposure images are taken very quickly with a high-speed camera and the clearest images are added up. <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261007\">Which object</a> in this <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smorgasbord\">sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord</a> interests you? Perhaps <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-30-waves-on-titan/\">Titan</a> or the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-25-fresh-tiger-stripes-on-saturns-enceladus/\">icy stripes of Enceladus</a>? Maybe the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-gap-moons/\">gaps</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-5-spokes-on-saturns-b-ring/\">spokes</a> in Saturn\u2019s rings? Saturn's <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/what-is-opposition-astronomy/\">opposition</a>, when Earth passes in between the planet and the Sun, occurred on October 4th. Due to the planet's proximity and full illumination from the Sun, now is a great time of year to observe it!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> bumpy", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tw.astrophotography/\">Tom Williams</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tw.astrophotography/\">Tom Williams</a>", "alt": "Saturn sits near the center of the image. The layers of different colored atmosphere are clear. Its large rings are tilted with respect to the image&#039;s horizontal axis. The gaps and spokes within the rings are clear. A few of Saturn&#039;s moons show up as faint dots surrounding the planet.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-8-the-saturn-system-smorgasbord/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026-09-21-2349_3-TW-RGB-Sat2_1.5x_Labelled.png?w=2296&h=1500&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 8 - The Saturn System Sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-8-the-saturn-system-smorgasbord/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: How was the Saturn system imaged so clearly? Astrophotographer Tom Williams captured such exquisite details due to one night of exceptional atmospheric conditions above the United Kingdom. This means more than a cloudless sky. Pockets of air at different temperatures and densities move around and bend light as it travels through Earth\u2019s atmosphere, distorting astronomy images. This is called \u201cseeing.\u201d Less atmospheric turbulence means clearer images. The astrophotographer reduced the impact of seeing with the lucky imaging technique: thousands of short-exposure images are taken very quickly with a high-speed camera and the clearest images are added up. Which object in this sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord interests you? Perhaps Titan or the icy stripes of Enceladus? Maybe the gaps and spokes in Saturn\u2019s rings? Saturn&#039;s opposition, when Earth passes in between the planet and the Sun, occurred on October 4th. Due to the planet&#039;s proximity and full illumination from the Sun, now is a great time of year to observe it!Tomorrow&#039;s picture: bumpy\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the saturn system sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Saturn System Sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-8-the-saturn-system-smorgasbord/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026-09-21-2349_3-TW-RGB-Sat2_1.5x_Labelled.png?w=2296&#038;h=1500&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026-09-21-2349_3-TW-RGB-Sat2_1.5x_Labelled.png?w=2296&#038;h=1500&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Saturn sits near the center of the image. The layers of different colored atmosphere are clear. Its large rings are tilted with respect to the image&#039;s horizontal axis. The gaps and spokes within the rings are clear. A few of Saturn&#039;s moons show up as faint dots surrounding the planet.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-08T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-08T08:39:13-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Saturn System Sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026-09-21-2349_3-TW-RGB-Sat2_1.5x_Labelled.png?w=2296&#038;h=1500&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 8<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026-09-21-2349_3-TW-RGB-Sat2_1.5x_Labelled.png\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/2026-09-21-2349_3-TW-RGB-Sat2_1.5x_Labelled.png\" alt=\"Saturn sits near the center of the image. The layers of different colored atmosphere are clear. Its large rings are tilted with respect to the image&#039;s horizontal axis. The gaps and spokes within the rings are clear. A few of Saturn&#039;s moons show up as faint dots surrounding the planet.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Saturn System Sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/tw.astrophotography/\">Tom Williams</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> How was the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/saturn/\">Saturn</a> system imaged so clearly? Astrophotographer Tom Williams captured such exquisite details due to one night of exceptional atmospheric conditions above the United Kingdom. This means more than a cloudless sky. Pockets of air at different temperatures and densities move around and <a href=\"http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/geoopt/refr.html\">bend light</a> as it travels through Earth\u2019s atmosphere, distorting astronomy images. This is called \u201c<a href=\"https://britastro.org/journal_contents_ite/absolute-beginners-no-14-astronomical-seeing\">seeing</a>.\u201d Less atmospheric turbulence means clearer images. The astrophotographer reduced the impact of seeing with the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucky_imaging\">lucky imaging</a> technique: thousands of short-exposure images are taken very quickly with a high-speed camera and the clearest images are added up. <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/discuss_apod.php?date=261007\">Which object</a> in this <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smorgasbord\">sm\u00f6rg\u00e5sbord</a> interests you? Perhaps <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-april-30-waves-on-titan/\">Titan</a> or the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-august-25-fresh-tiger-stripes-on-saturns-enceladus/\">icy stripes of Enceladus</a>? Maybe the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-gap-moons/\">gaps</a> and <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-august-5-spokes-on-saturns-b-ring/\">spokes</a> in Saturn\u2019s rings? Saturn's <a href=\"https://earthsky.org/astronomy-essentials/what-is-opposition-astronomy/\">opposition</a>, when Earth passes in between the planet and the Sun, occurred on October 4th. Due to the planet's proximity and full illumination from the Sun, now is a great time of year to observe it!<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> bumpy\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-7-supernova-remnant-pa-30/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261008/html"}